00:03:53.922 --> 00:03:54.368
MELANIE WARRICK: --on AutoML.

00:03:54.368 --> 00:03:55.367
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.650
So she has another great
article talking about AutoML.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.200
But in this case, it's talking
about AutoML Translate, which

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.450
is really kind of interesting.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.130
It talks about how and
why you might need AutoML

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.090
power translate capabilities.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.600
So the example that they
use is like, for example,

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.740
using the word "driver."

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.032
Like, if you're playing golf,
"driver" means one thing.

00:04:12.032 --> 00:04:14.281
But if you need a taxi,
"driver" means something else.

00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:15.940
But if you're in a
software context,

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.429
"driver" means
yet another thing.

00:04:17.429 --> 00:04:19.220
And so you might need
something like AutoML

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.144
to be able to do translations
that are domain-specific.

00:04:22.144 --> 00:04:24.560
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, and she
steps you through an example

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.140
that she spelt out specifically
with AutoML Translate,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.600
and also using something
known as Welocalize,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.054
which is-- it's one of
the largest localization

00:04:32.054 --> 00:04:33.470
providers in the
US with a mission

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.290
to help the customers localize
content for global audiences.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.840
So you can check out
her article and see how

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.545
to use Google AutoML Translate.

00:04:40.545 --> 00:04:41.420
