00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.150
It was actually really,
really good talk.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:37.539
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.850
Mark, what got you starting
to come to Strange Loop?

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:41.679
MARK MANDEL: I used to come
here from Australia regularly,

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:42.940
I think, about six years ago.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:44.320
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow, so
you've been here for six years

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:44.710
as well?

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:45.640
MARK MANDEL:
Something like that.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.260
I think I missed a year
somewhere in between.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:47.960
I'm trying to remember.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.870
I think I had a friend who
was coming, or I saw it.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:51.560
And I was like, that
just looks awesome,

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.060
and I need to be
at that conference.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.049
And so I started, I think,
is the short answer.

00:08:55.049 --> 00:08:56.590
And then I was like,
this is amazing.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:57.630
I need to come back.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:00.404
It's a conference that
imposes imposter syndrome.

00:09:00.404 --> 00:09:02.381
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:02.381 --> 00:09:03.130
Like, it doesn't--

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.062
MELANIE WARRICK: That sounds
really inspiring for people

00:09:04.062 --> 00:09:04.480
to come.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:05.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I know.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:05.980
But I feel like it's true.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.840
Because it is an environment
in which you're always

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.490
going to meet someone who
knows stuff you don't know.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:10.540
Or you're just going
to sit in a talk

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.280
and just be like, I have
no idea what that is.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.870
But I feel like, two
years down the line,

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.220
if you head in a
certain direction,

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:16.886
depending on what it
is, you'll be like,

00:09:16.886 --> 00:09:18.810
oh, I remember that
talk from Strange Loop

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:20.770
two years ago that I saw
about someone talking

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:21.820
about something.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.520
Now I know how to make
mathematical models that'll

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:25.960
tell me how to
fold origami paper.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:27.340
Like, awesome, I can use that.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:28.006
[GONG]

00:09:28.006 --> 00:09:29.630
MELANIE WARRICK: And
did you make that?

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.296
MARK MANDEL: I have
not needed that yet.

00:09:31.296 --> 00:09:33.410
But I assume, at some
point, that I will.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.070
MELANIE WARRICK:
Someday, you will.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:36.290
OK, well, if it's imposing
the imposter syndrome,

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:38.090
why are you both
coming back so often?

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.048
KF: I don't know that I
have imposter syndrome.

00:09:40.048 --> 00:09:41.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:44.077
I think I just tend to see
working in the tech industry

00:09:44.077 --> 00:09:46.160
as being like in many other
jobs in which you just

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.740
have to constantly pick up new
skills, because capitalism.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:49.448
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:09:49.448 --> 00:09:50.150
I also love to--

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.170
I love to learn.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:54.700
That's a big, deep-seeded
thing that I love, a passion.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:55.700
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.130
I appreciate that
this conference

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.380
seems to be very driven to give
conference talks on knowledge

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.810
and sharing knowledge
verses, like, we

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.250
want to show you the new--

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:04.610
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:04.850
MELANIE WARRICK: --fandangled--

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:05.690
MARK MANDEL: It's
very much not a--

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.080
MELANIE WARRICK: --shiny--

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:06.470
MARK MANDEL:
--beginner conference--

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:06.830
MELANIE WARRICK: --thing.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:07.640
MARK MANDEL: --but,
I think, is still

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.890
open to people who are
beginners, either levels

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:10.550
or mindsets.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:11.210
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.620
KF: Whenever I started
coming, I had, like,

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:13.761
no experience programming.

00:10:13.761 --> 00:10:14.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:16.660
Wait, is that like
realistically or functionally?

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.935
KF: No, like realistically, I
had no experience programming.

00:10:19.935 --> 00:10:22.310
I had, like, three months
of experience programming

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.660
or something ridiculous.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:25.993
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I'd never
done functional programming

00:10:25.993 --> 00:10:28.160
before or touched anything
that had been mentioned

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.570
at Strange Loop before I came.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:31.070
KF: In the first
year I came, it was

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.470
before they had opportunity
grants and things.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:34.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Which is
one of the things I've always

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:35.720
been very impressed with.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:36.700
With this conference
in particular,

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.033
there's been such a drive,
specifically with opportunity

00:10:39.033 --> 00:10:41.990
grants, and just making
sure that the diversity is

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.760
being recognized and built out.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.510
And if anything, I've seen
that evolve and change.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.260
It's such a small
thing, but it's so true.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:49.340
Like, last year
was the first time

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.900
I saw a line at the
women's bathroom.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:52.040
And it was the first
time I've ever seen

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:53.110
a line at the women's bathroom.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:54.560
I was like, oh, that's awesome.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.800
Any other highlights from
the conference this year,

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:58.640
previous years?

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.752
Like, did you all meet
at this conference?

00:11:00.752 --> 00:11:01.960
MARK MANDEL: How did we meet?

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:05.270
KF: We met through our mutual
friend Sean, who you know

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.220
from when you did ColdFusion--

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:10.610
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds about right.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.060
That sounds about
right, actually.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.452
KF: --and who I know through
things unrelated to ColdFusion.

00:11:15.452 --> 00:11:17.660
MARK MANDEL: You weren't a
ColdFusion programmer too?

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.094
Actually, to your
point-- like, we

00:11:19.094 --> 00:11:20.510
were talking about
why I was here.

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:22.580
I feel like-- and if we want
to go back through my history,

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:23.300
because I don't know why.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.740
That seems like a
thing we're doing now--

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:26.364
I think it was around
the time that I'd

00:11:26.364 --> 00:11:28.310
stopped doing
ColdFusion as a thing

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:30.205
and was really trying
to expand my horizons

00:11:30.205 --> 00:11:31.580
and learn a whole
bunch of stuff.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.705
And Strange Loop looked
like an amazing opportunity

00:11:33.705 --> 00:11:35.750
to do that, and honestly,
continues to be so.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.125
MELANIE WARRICK:
Did Strange Loop

00:11:37.125 --> 00:11:40.400
inspire you to get more
exposure to distributed

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.680
systems, distributed computing?

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.970
KF: I think it sort of
came the other way around,

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.080
which is, I got into
programming because I

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.430
was in grad school for math.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:52.920
And for some assignment my
first semester of grad school,

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:58.760
I ended up reading research
papers from the Google research

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:02.630
website, and was
reading something

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:05.187
about clustering algorithms
at YouTube or something,

00:12:05.187 --> 00:12:06.770
and was like, oh,
this is interesting,

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:08.870
and then tried to learn
to program and stuff,

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.330
and then ended up
going to Code School.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.670
And then once I
was there, people

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.280
were like, you seem to
be the kind of person who

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.939
enjoys academic papers,
which means, probably,

00:12:18.939 --> 00:12:20.855
functional programming
and distributed systems

00:12:20.855 --> 00:12:22.139
are for you.

00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:23.930
MELANIE WARRICK: And
then you went with it?

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:29.566
KF: Yeah-- not necessarily a
normal start into programming.

00:12:29.566 --> 00:12:31.940
MELANIE WARRICK: What is a
normal start into programming?

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:35.360
KF: I do know that when I
started out doing programming

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.030
and could cite a bunch
of academic papers

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:40.220
but had no experience
in industry,

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:41.580
that was deeply unusual.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.500
And people did not know
how to respond to it.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.910
I think it's less bizarre
now, in part, I think,

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:48.950
because they have things
like Strange Loop that

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:49.950
have opportunity grants.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:51.500
So it's not unheard of.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.717
MARK MANDEL: Do you
recommend it as a pathway?

00:12:53.717 --> 00:12:55.425
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
a great question.

00:12:55.425 --> 00:12:56.716
KF: Recommend is a strong word.

00:12:56.716 --> 00:13:00.770
I would say follow
your bliss also though.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.950
I don't think it's harder,
from a technical perspective,

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.570
to pick up these things.

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:09.410
There's nothing inherent about
this area of computer science

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.150
or software
engineering that I feel

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.580
is harder than doing
front end, for example.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.550
I think that sometimes people
make it artificially harder,

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:18.050
because they think it's harder.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:20.180
So they don't
bother to learn how

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.690
to talk about things in a
way that's more approachable

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:26.690
versus, does there
exist something that

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.850
teaches distributed
systems to beginners

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:33.030
in a systematic way like the
way that there's Girls Who Code.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.820
And what are the
nonprofit, like--

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:35.310
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.190
KF: There are so many though, at
this point, where they teach--

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:37.940
MELANIE WARRICK: I
know what you mean.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:38.860
KF: There are so many places--

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:39.330
MARK MANDEL: The variety.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.555
KF: --where you can--
yes, the variety--

00:13:40.555 --> 00:13:41.350
MELANIE WARRICK: Where you can--

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:41.650
KF: --of all these--

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:42.020
MELANIE WARRICK: You can learn--

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:42.220
KF: --workshop series.

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:43.430
MELANIE WARRICK:
--to build a website.

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:44.610
You can learn how to build a--

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:44.810
KF: Yeah.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:46.893
MELANIE WARRICK: --a machine
learning application.

00:13:46.893 --> 00:13:49.780
You haven't seen anything yet
that's been introductory to--

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:51.620
KF: No, I don't
think people see it

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.810
as being introductory
at all, in part

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:56.752
because nobody writes
introductory materials.

00:13:56.752 --> 00:13:59.210
MELANIE WARRICK: Where does
distributed computing typically

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:03.050
sit within the structure
of an engineering org

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.390
from what you've seen and
what you've experienced?

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:09.140
KF: I tend to get bounced around
between back end infrastructure

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:10.880
platform-type teams.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:14.240
And then I sort of fell
into doing data things

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.610
for a few years,
like off and on.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:19.580
Because a lot of
the languages that I

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:23.660
was using to do platform-type
stuff were also starting to get

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:25.430
used for data stuff.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:29.200
So like Scala, originally, I
picked up not for data stuff.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:32.330
I picked it up primarily
because functional programming

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:33.670
and distributed systems.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.190
And then that started becoming
a language, because of Spark,

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:37.534
that people were using for data.

00:14:37.534 --> 00:14:38.950
So then people
looked at my resume

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:40.360
and were like, you
do data things,

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:41.985
because you have a
math degree and know

00:14:41.985 --> 00:14:44.860
these languages, which is not
necessarily accurate, though it

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:45.797
is now.

00:14:45.797 --> 00:14:48.130
MELANIE WARRICK: That's kind
of how it works in general.

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.555
But I remember, at
one point, you also--

00:14:50.555 --> 00:14:52.706
you've had a passion
for Clojure, Clojure

00:14:52.706 --> 00:14:54.580
in terms of the functional
programming space.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:54.970
MARK MANDEL: With a J.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:55.850
MELANIE WARRICK: I know that's--

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:56.310
yes.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:57.550
MARK MANDEL: Just making
sure that's clear.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:14:59.150
MELANIE WARRICK:
I appreciate that.

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:03.670
KF: Yeah, so I fell into
doing Clojure not for work,

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:05.772
but because I was
on a mailing list.

00:15:05.772 --> 00:15:08.230
And Bridget, who's now one of
the co-organizers for Strange

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.480
Loop, was putting
together workshops

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:13.540
to teach women Clojure and--
or functional programming

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:14.560
through Clojure.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.500
And before I worked in
tech, I worked in teaching.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:20.620
So I was sort of like, if you
need any help with curriculum

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.730
development, let me know.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:24.700
And then I got involved
with it through that.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:26.650
And then eventually,
despite the fact

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:30.170
that I had not written almost
any Clojure in earnest,

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.560
I just became very
strongly associated with it

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.970
until it was actually
a thing that I did.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.512
MARK MANDEL: So I
also want to know--

00:15:36.512 --> 00:15:38.345
I'm very curious about
this answer actually.

00:15:38.345 --> 00:15:38.936
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:38.936 --> 00:15:40.415
We've said distributed
computing-- you

00:15:40.415 --> 00:15:41.331
like disputed systems.

00:15:41.331 --> 00:15:42.880
What does that mean to you?

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:46.349
KF: I think that there is a
meaning that it holds for me,

00:15:46.349 --> 00:15:48.640
and then there's the meaning
that it holds in industry.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:50.598
MARK MANDEL: And what's
the-- yeah, what is it?

00:15:50.598 --> 00:15:53.510
KF: Because to me, if you're
a front end developer,

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:55.540
you have to have ideas
about distributed

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:57.220
computing at this point.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.510
I think when you talk
to people in industry,

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.830
most of the time, what they mean
is you work on core platform

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.140
infrastructure-type deals.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:07.150
MARK MANDEL: Does
that mean Kubernetes?

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.394
KF: Amongst other things, sure.

00:16:09.394 --> 00:16:10.810
MELANIE WARRICK:
Mark really wants

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.435
to bring up the
Kubernetes perspective,

00:16:12.435 --> 00:16:14.110
because I believe
there was some stuff

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:15.490
in the history between
the two of the two of you

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:16.239
around Kubernetes.

00:16:16.239 --> 00:16:18.610
KF: When I met Mark, he was
still living in Australia,

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.830
and did not work for Google--

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:19.990
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:22.559
KF: --and had no reason to
shill Kubernetes at the time.

00:16:22.559 --> 00:16:23.350
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:24.850
KF: It wasn't even
necessarily popular--

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:25.516
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:16:25.516 --> 00:16:26.368
KF: --at the time.

00:16:26.368 --> 00:16:26.774
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh.

00:16:26.774 --> 00:16:27.774
MARK MANDEL: But I got--

00:16:27.774 --> 00:16:29.577
KF: But he was deeply
enthusiastic in a way

00:16:29.577 --> 00:16:31.910
where everybody was like, why
don't you just go and sell

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:33.320
people Kubernetes as your job?

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:35.820
And then he became a
developer relations person

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.250
for Kubernetes things.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:37.780
MELANIE WARRICK:
And here we are.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:38.300
MARK MANDEL: And here we are.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.080
But now you've actually
used Kubernetes

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.410
after giving me grief about
it for a really long time.

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:42.810
KF: Sure.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:43.450
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:44.700
MELANIE WARRICK: And what
do you think of Kubernetes?

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.158
KF: And now we've
come full circle.

00:16:46.158 --> 00:16:47.155
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yes.

00:16:47.155 --> 00:16:49.120
MELANIE WARRICK: And what
do you think of Kubernetes?

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:50.494
KF: I think it's
a tool, and it's

00:16:50.494 --> 00:16:52.100
a step in the right direction.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:55.120
And I think it helps
build better abstractions

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.440
in the long term.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:57.940
I tend to think
about these things

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.950
not necessarily
from the perspective

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:02.800
of running infrastructure
for the sake of keeping

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.369
things running, but more from
the idea of infrastructure

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:07.413
as a product.

00:17:07.413 --> 00:17:08.829
And I think that
it becomes easier

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:10.359
to build those
kinds of tools when

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.317
you have something like
Kubernetes that becomes

00:17:12.317 --> 00:17:13.442
an abstraction over things.

00:17:13.442 --> 00:17:15.941
MELANIE WARRICK: Are there any
other tools that you've found

00:17:15.941 --> 00:17:17.530
are valuable in the
work that you do?

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:20.380
KF: I mean, there's Docker,
which is just sort of,

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:22.390
at this point, also
prolific after--

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.752
like, it wasn't when I started.

00:17:23.752 --> 00:17:25.210
And now after five
years, it's just

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.594
like, I can't imagine not
using it at this point.

00:17:28.594 --> 00:17:30.510
MELANIE WARRICK: So I
know we've talked around

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:32.343
about the different
things that you've done.

00:17:32.343 --> 00:17:35.590
What do you enjoy doing
in the computing space?

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:38.650
KF: I don't know that I enjoy
specific technical things

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:39.250
at this point.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:40.840
I enjoy building
things that are useful.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:41.923
MARK MANDEL: That's valid.

00:17:41.923 --> 00:17:44.860
Because I think you've had
kind of a history of building

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.990
platforms that
would then be on use

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.160
by other areas of companies.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:49.700
Would that be vaguely right?

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:50.200
KF: Yeah.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.140
I haven't done as much
feature development for things

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:54.760
that get used externally.

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:56.320
I've done some of that.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:58.300
When I work on those
kinds of services,

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:00.754
it's usually more from
an SRE perspective

00:18:00.754 --> 00:18:02.170
where I'm trying
to make something

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:03.544
more performant
for the customer,

00:18:03.544 --> 00:18:05.985
not necessarily building
the initial thing.

00:18:05.985 --> 00:18:07.360
MELANIE WARRICK:
So I know you've

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:08.470
done the coding bootcamp.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.300
You've done some of
your own self-study.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:13.260
What do you think are
some good pathways now--

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.210
and we already talked about some
of the new coding projects that

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:18.220
are out there a little bit in
terms of getting people in.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:20.303
But what do you think are
some good ways for those

00:18:20.303 --> 00:18:22.990
who are trying to find their
pathway into building out

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:26.350
things, building things,
whatever form that takes?

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.330
KF: I think all of
the options are hard.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.150
I think there was a
period of time where,

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:33.610
if you already had a degree,
or you couldn't afford

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:37.840
to go to college, then coding
bootcamps were potentially

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:40.586
a really great option if
you chose the right one.

00:18:40.586 --> 00:18:43.210
But now they've been around for
so long, and there are so many,

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:45.010
I think it's hard to choose one.

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.770
Because there are so many
that are potentially bad.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:50.320
And there's no
regulation on them.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.650
If you don't have experience
with software engineering

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:55.120
and you maybe don't
know anyone who

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.760
does software engineering,
which is the kind of person who

00:18:57.760 --> 00:18:59.560
I think would go to
a coding bootcamp,

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:02.170
it's kind of impossible to tell
whether something is snake oil

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:03.010
or not.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.750
So I say that coding bootcamps
exist, and some of them are OK.

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:10.810
And probably check Course
Report, see if it's decent.

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:13.042
There are more places
that are starting

00:19:13.042 --> 00:19:14.500
to do apprenticeships,
which I feel

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.810
are always the best option.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.180
If you can manage to go
to school for computer

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:23.630
science, that's always, I
think, still the best option.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:25.584
But that's not really
realistic for everyone.

00:19:25.584 --> 00:19:26.500
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:28.000
KF: It wasn't realistic for me.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.880
MELANIE WARRICK: Well actually,
the keynote this morning

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.963
at Strange Loop was talking
about how important it

00:19:31.963 --> 00:19:35.171
is for all kids to be exposed to
some type of computer science,

00:19:35.171 --> 00:19:36.420
or just basically, something--

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:36.680
MARK MANDEL: Foundational--

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:36.940
MELANIE WARRICK: --around--

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:37.595
MARK MANDEL: --computer
science, yeah.

00:19:37.595 --> 00:19:39.040
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah,
something along those lines,

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.502
as well as trying to give
ideas to think outside the box.

00:19:41.502 --> 00:19:43.210
But what do you think
about that in terms

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:44.950
of the education of kids?

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.200
Do you think that that's a
skill that definitely needs

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.460
to be applied across the board?

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:50.376
KF: I think that everyone
should have exposure

00:19:50.376 --> 00:19:51.850
to computer science.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:54.460
I think some people take
it to a larger degree

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:58.360
and say everyone should have so
many years of computer science

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:00.190
classes or coding
classes in school.

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:02.830
I don't agree that
that's reasonable.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:05.590
Having worked in schools, it's
like, there's not enough time

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:06.880
already to cover things.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:08.380
Like, what is the
thing you're going

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:11.170
to take out of the
curriculum to put this in?

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:14.830
I would struggle to think of
something like a core subject,

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.770
if you're treating computer
science as a core subject, that

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:20.020
is less important
than learning to code.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:21.800
I would struggle to
think of something.

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:22.420
MELANIE WARRICK: And
one of the points

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:25.630
they were making this morning
too is how it's hard to find,

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.050
also, teachers, specifically
showing some numbers

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:31.030
in terms of there's
less teachers

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.932
to be able to provide
the education.

00:20:32.932 --> 00:20:34.390
MARK MANDEL: And
the incentives are

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:36.389
aligned for people with
computer science degrees

00:20:36.389 --> 00:20:37.660
to not be teachers.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:37.990
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:38.970
And I was thinking at the time--

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:40.150
I was like, you
could line that up

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.649
against those who
have the expertise

00:20:41.649 --> 00:20:43.100
in the machine learning space.

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:44.830
And I'm sure it's
even less people.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:46.990
But I guess the
question I'd ask is--

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:49.420
you've mentored a number of
people coming into the space.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.100
Do you have any
advice for those who

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.860
might be thinking about being
mentors, who want to help

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:55.620
others getting into the space?

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:58.540
KF: I think the main
thing is that you should

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.300
try to, one, align yourself
with some sort of nonprofit

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:03.630
if you're doing
it systematically.

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:06.910
There are coding bootcamps
that are for profit that

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.100
rely very heavily on mentors.

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:11.800
And if the reason
for mentoring people

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:15.310
is to help get more
people into the field,

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:17.290
I don't understand the
rationale for doing that

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:19.300
at a for-profit school.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:22.630
In the same way that
I will sometimes

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:25.450
go give talks at universities,
to college students,

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.490
about computer
science, if I'm going

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:30.550
to sink a bunch of
my time connecting

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.760
with some sort of organization
that's providing mentorship

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.740
to people, I tend
to go for something

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:37.390
that's low-cost or free.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:40.000
Because that's usually
people who need it most.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.620
A lot of people I have met
through going to conferences,

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.800
or being involved
specifically with setting up

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:50.740
opportunity grant systems,
or volunteering around that.

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:55.480
I don't know that this is advice
for people mentoring so much

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:58.979
as for people who need help with
things, as you'd be surprised

00:21:58.979 --> 00:22:01.270
how willing people are to
help you if you just ask them

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:02.080
for it.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:04.295
Most of the time
people will say yes,

00:22:04.295 --> 00:22:06.670
not because I asked for help,
but because people asked me

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:07.390
for help.

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:10.190
And most of the time I say yes.

00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:13.900
Or I introduce them to someone
who's more able to help them.

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.947
And most of the time,
that person says yes.

00:22:15.947 --> 00:22:17.530
MARK MANDEL: I was
going to say, yeah,

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:19.917
have you got any suggestions
for people to find mentors?

00:22:19.917 --> 00:22:22.000
I know that's been-- that
can be tricky sometimes.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:22.630
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:24.088
MARK MANDEL: Just
ask the question?

00:22:24.088 --> 00:22:26.020
KF: I'd ask the
question, but also

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:28.750
spend some amount of
time upfront trying

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:31.600
to figure out who the person is
and what they're specialty is.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:31.810
MARK MANDEL: So
you're not saying

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:34.309
I should just hop on LinkedIn
and spam everyone with emails?

00:22:34.309 --> 00:22:36.036
KF: No, that's not
a good approach.

00:22:36.036 --> 00:22:37.369
MARK MANDEL: That's not a thing.

00:22:37.369 --> 00:22:40.420
KF: It's more like, go to events
in your area if you have them.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:43.090
If you don't-- every
job I've gotten,

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:44.480
I've gotten through Twitter.

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:49.420
And most of the people who
I know who have spent time

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:53.260
with me teaching me things, a
disproportionately large number

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:55.660
of those people
I met on Twitter.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:57.652
It's more like, go
socialize with people.

00:22:57.652 --> 00:22:59.110
MARK MANDEL: That
seems reasonable.

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:00.620
KF: I'm originally from Houston.

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:03.100
So the advice of,
like, go to events

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:05.890
doesn't apply as strongly
in some cities as in others.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.080
Because there aren't as
many tech events in Houston

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:10.686
as there are even in
Portland, where I live now.

00:23:10.686 --> 00:23:12.310
MELANIE WARRICK: Or
start up your own--

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:12.595
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:23:12.595 --> 00:23:12.880
MELANIE WARRICK: --event.

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:14.860
And start up your own
community if you can.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:17.810
MARK MANDEL: That takes work,
but yeah, if you've got time.

00:23:17.810 --> 00:23:18.480
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's another way.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.780
I know-- I've met a number
of people who have done that.

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:22.930
And I know you and I have talked
about this before, but just

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:24.970
to be on the same page
on this one, the people

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:26.140
you've mentored
especially-- like,

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:27.520
not everybody has a math degree.

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:29.186
Not everybody who's
come into this space

00:23:29.186 --> 00:23:32.505
necessarily had much experience
with computer science,

00:23:32.505 --> 00:23:33.880
the people that
I've come across,

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:35.430
and I know, for myself, too.

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:37.905
Would you say that's probably
what you've seen as well?

00:23:37.905 --> 00:23:39.030
KF: Yeah, most of the time.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:40.690
MELANIE WARRICK: Because I
know that that can sometimes

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:42.700
be an intimidating factor,
talking about the imposter

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:43.310
syndrome before.

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:45.393
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and
it's probably worth noting,

00:23:45.393 --> 00:23:46.960
I mean, amongst our
peers and people

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:49.600
I know, I know a variety
people who have degrees,

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:51.910
but not even remotely
in computer science,

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:53.620
in psychology, in philosophy.

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:55.930
And I know people who are
theater majors, right,

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:57.230
who work in computer science.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:58.930
KF: My first degree
was in English.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:01.000
And then I went back to school
later and got a math degree.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:02.740
MARK MANDEL: Technically, I
have a bachelor in multimedia.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.260
I don't know what
that's worth at all.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:05.006
SPEAKER 1: I have a film degree.

00:24:05.006 --> 00:24:05.380
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:05.965
KF: It's for making podcasts.

00:24:05.965 --> 00:24:07.174
That's what it's for.

00:24:07.174 --> 00:24:08.130
MARK MANDEL: That's
what it's for?

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:09.800
It's so I can use
Macromedia Director.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:10.900
That's what it's for.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.110
MELANIE WARRICK: That
is the only reason why.

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:12.240
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:13.270
MELANIE WARRICK:
Yeah, how did games

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:14.500
come into the middle of this?

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:15.625
Just because you play them.

00:24:15.625 --> 00:24:16.974
MARK MANDEL: I play them.

00:24:16.974 --> 00:24:18.640
MELANIE WARRICK: So
on a different note,

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.065
how do you get
all your work done

00:24:20.065 --> 00:24:21.190
with your cat on your head?

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:22.060
[CAT MEOWING]

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.300
KF: I work from home.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.610
My cat usually leaves
me alone unless I'm

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:28.877
in meetings is the key thing.

00:24:28.877 --> 00:24:30.210
MARK MANDEL: Sounds about right.

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:31.870
MELANIE WARRICK:
How do you make sure

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:34.305
you're successful in what
you're doing working remotely.

00:24:34.305 --> 00:24:36.795
Because I know that's something
that you typically do.

00:24:36.795 --> 00:24:39.100
KF: Yeah, I've always
worked remotely.

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:41.202
Or for a while, I
worked for a company

00:24:41.202 --> 00:24:43.160
that was in Portland
while I lived in Portland,

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.690
but just never went
into the office.

00:24:47.455 --> 00:24:51.010
I think I started doing remote
stuff from when I was entry

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:53.950
level on, which is unusual.

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:56.530
Because most people aren't
willing to hire people who are

00:24:56.530 --> 00:24:58.360
entry level to work remotely.

00:24:58.360 --> 00:25:01.750
I think it was mostly
a factor of finding

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:03.340
support systems locally--

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:05.920
or even if not
locally, like now,

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.190
all the time, I'll
run into something.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:11.650
And I won't really know
how to solve the problem.

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:13.960
And I'll be like the tech
lead, so there's no one else

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:15.440
to solve it for me at work.

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.950
So I end up going
and talking to people

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.290
I just know from the internet--

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:21.100
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:22.890
KF: --or people I met in
other cities when I was

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:24.340
at conferences, or
when I lived there,

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:26.080
or when I worked for a
company there and be like--

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:26.620
MARK MANDEL: In your network.

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:27.828
KF: --have you run into this?

00:25:27.828 --> 00:25:28.330
Yeah.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:29.470
And then we do that
for each other.

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:30.560
MARK MANDEL: That's a
really good question too,

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.500
in just talking about
remote culture and something

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:34.690
I've-- like you've said people
don't like to hire people who

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.550
are at entry level
for remote positions.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:38.350
What's a successful
way of bringing

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:40.725
in entry-level people for
remote positions, do you think?

00:25:40.725 --> 00:25:42.933
KF: I don't think it's a
requirement that people have

00:25:42.933 --> 00:25:45.115
previous professional
experience to work remotely

00:25:45.115 --> 00:25:46.240
as part of their first job.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:49.660
Because I've worked with
entry-level people who

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.490
had never really
had a job before

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.760
and were remote in other
time zones, other countries,

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:56.180
and it worked out fine.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:26:00.130
I do think, though, that it
is helpful to set them up

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:04.687
with a specific person who
can walk them through--

00:26:04.687 --> 00:26:06.270
MARK MANDEL: Like a
one-on-one mentor.

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:07.394
KF: --what that looks like.

00:26:07.394 --> 00:26:10.540
Yeah, you should do that anyway
when someone is starting a job,

00:26:10.540 --> 00:26:12.820
but even more so
with remote stuff,

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:15.370
especially if you can
find someone who's

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:18.720
been doing remote for a while.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:23.110
And it helps to be very explicit
about what your day looks like

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:24.880
and what other people's
days look like.

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:26.600
So when I started
working remotely,

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:28.960
I did not actually have
industry experience.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:31.210
Before that, I'd only
ever worked in schools.

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:34.403
So I did not understand what it
was like to work in an office.

00:26:34.403 --> 00:26:35.270
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:36.280
KF: So I had this--

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:38.590
I didn't understand
that there were

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:41.830
times of the day when people
went up to go get coffee,

00:26:41.830 --> 00:26:44.530
or walk down the street
to get coffee even.

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.170
And that was like a
20-minute break right there.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:49.197
So I had this
weird notion that I

00:26:49.197 --> 00:26:51.280
needed to be sitting at
my computer all the time--

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:52.000
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:54.040
KF: --or like-- and I
needed to put it, like,

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:54.880
eight hours a day.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:57.190
And I also worked
freelance for a while--

00:26:57.190 --> 00:27:01.461
like eight hours a day of
earnest coding, not working,

00:27:01.461 --> 00:27:01.960
which--

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:03.010
MELANIE WARRICK: And
then a coffee break.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:05.750
KF: Yeah, now I understand that
working involves many things,

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:07.150
such as talking to people.

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:08.142
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:27:08.142 --> 00:27:10.410
KF: When I started out,
I didn't understand this.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:13.750
And I would just anchor myself
to my computer for eight hours

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:15.920
a day minimum and code things.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:17.680
And I remember
co-workers would try

00:27:17.680 --> 00:27:20.595
to encouraged me to get lunch.

00:27:20.595 --> 00:27:22.720
MARK MANDEL: Right, which
is a thing you should do.

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:23.680
KF: You should do this.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:24.388
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:27:24.388 --> 00:27:25.750
KF: But also, I was entry level.

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:28.180
And it was, like, my first
office, quote, unquote, "job."

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:28.971
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:27:28.971 --> 00:27:32.030
KF: You can't look around
and see other people modeling

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:33.000
how to do things.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:35.350
So you have to be very
explicit about, like, this

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:37.000
is how I conduct my day.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:40.150
You could also do it these other
ways, but this is how I do it.

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.258
MELANIE WARRICK: How do
you conduct your day?

00:27:42.258 --> 00:27:44.194
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:44.194 --> 00:27:47.630
KF: So I usually
start working by 9:30,

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:52.160
which means I get up at 9:00
at the earliest, sometimes.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.894
And then I shower and have
breakfast or whatever.

00:27:54.894 --> 00:27:56.560
I usually work from
home in the morning,

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:59.920
because I don't like commuting
during rush hour times.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:03.280
So unless I'm biking, I will
work from home in the morning.

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:07.190
And then I'll take lunch
at around noon to 1:00.

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:08.920
So I'll eat lunch
and then commute

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:10.660
into a co-working space.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:13.300
And then I will work from the
co-working space for the rest

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:17.860
of the afternoon and then come
home whenever about eight hours

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:20.960
of working has been
accomplished, or until--

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:23.830
sometimes I have a
mindset that I'm in,

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:25.180
and I just don't want to stop.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:27.610
So I'll keep working until I
finish the thing I'm doing.

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:28.966
MELANIE WARRICK: Adjust later?

00:28:28.966 --> 00:28:29.420
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.900
We've done this remote stuff,
which I actually really enjoy,

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:33.566
because it's something
I used to do too.

00:28:33.566 --> 00:28:36.760
I'd love to also hear about, how
do you find good communication

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:39.130
patterns in remote teams?

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:40.540
Things tend to be asynchronous.

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:42.290
Not everyone's awake
at the same time.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:44.581
Do you have thoughts on either
technology, or patterns,

00:28:44.581 --> 00:28:45.940
or workplace standards--

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:46.850
I don't know.

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:49.210
KF: I try to look for
people who enjoy talking.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:51.430
When I interview, I
try to see if they're

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:52.930
reasonably talkative.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.270
If they have an online
presence, that's

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:59.380
one way to tell if they tend
to talk a lot and stuff online.

00:28:59.380 --> 00:29:03.280
Despite working remotely
for the entire time

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:08.210
I've worked in tech, I am
not a very insular person.

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:09.160
And I talk--

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:09.950
[LAUGHING]

00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:10.490
MARK MANDEL: Shocked.

00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:10.800
KF: You're like--

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:11.200
MARK MANDEL: Shocked.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:11.825
KF: --shocking.

00:29:11.825 --> 00:29:12.817
Shocking, I know.

00:29:12.817 --> 00:29:14.650
MELANIE WARRICK: You
might have a few tweets

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.930
out there, maybe just a couple.

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:21.130
KF: So I try to find workplaces
where people aren't completely

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:23.320
siloed, and they still
talk to each other

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:24.800
all the time throughout the day.

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:27.880
I comically refer to it as
like, when I start a new job,

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:28.911
I'm joining a new slack.

00:29:28.911 --> 00:29:29.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.480
And do you feel
like you almost have

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:33.792
to be more vocal in
some ways, being remote?

00:29:33.792 --> 00:29:35.500
You kind of have to
push your personality

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:37.180
and your communication
out more than you would,

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:39.010
say, if you're in an office,
because of the distance?

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:40.150
KF: I have experienced
this especially

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:41.920
with people who
are starting out.

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:44.140
And they're relatively junior.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:46.150
And they are working
remotely for the first time

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:46.870
or something.

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:50.470
If you're in an office and
you have a junior dev who

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:52.474
is struggling, you
can usually tell

00:29:52.474 --> 00:29:54.640
that they're struggling,
because they look stressed,

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:56.800
and they haven't
moved for hours,

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:00.160
versus, if they're
remote, you have no idea

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:03.190
unless you constantly
ping them, or unless they

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:05.870
become very direct and tell
you that this is happening,

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:08.050
which is like extremely
unlikely that they

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:10.720
will do this unless you set
up that rapport in advance.

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:12.420
So yes.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:15.040
MARK MANDEL: And a lot of
GIFs, animated GIFs, I feel,

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:15.670
help a lot.

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:21.174
KF: I use more GIFs
than are reasonable.

00:30:21.174 --> 00:30:22.840
MELANIE WARRICK: On
a more general note,

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:25.320
what do you value most in the
companies that you work for?

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:30.010
KF: I try to find people
who are different from me,

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:32.080
who are sufficiently
different from me

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:35.830
that I'm not just
stagnating as a person.

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:38.500
I think a lot of
people look for jobs

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.510
where you're picking
up a new tech stack

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:43.360
and pushing yourself
in that regard.

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:46.360
I try to find
companies where they're

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:49.300
open to hiring junior devs,
where not everyone is white,

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:51.610
where not everyone is a
man, where there are queer

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:54.710
people, where there are
people with disabilities,

00:30:54.710 --> 00:30:57.080
especially because
if you hire remote,

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:01.960
I feel like a lot of companies
miss the part about how

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:03.970
that kind of
environment can be--

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:06.190
like, when it's
set up correctly,

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:08.650
give people really
great job opportunities.

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:10.750
Because people who are
disabled are generally

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:11.710
deeply unemployed.

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:13.510
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:14.980
So this is great.

00:31:14.980 --> 00:31:17.050
And I just wanted
to say, is there

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:20.350
anything else that you wanted to
talk about around Strange Loop,

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:22.250
around working
remotely, around--

00:31:22.250 --> 00:31:23.250
MARK MANDEL: Kubernetes?

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:24.791
Do you want to talk
about Kubernetes?

00:31:24.791 --> 00:31:28.240
MELANIE WARRICK: --Kubernetes,
where you're going to be next?

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:29.380
KF: Not particularly.

00:31:29.380 --> 00:31:29.987
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:29.987 --> 00:31:31.570
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's a good answer.

