00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:46.111
Anyway, so the
question of the week--

00:32:46.111 --> 00:32:49.360
how do I encrypt and decrypt
data with Cloud Key Management

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:50.130
Service?

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:51.790
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I think this
is actually really interesting.

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:53.140
I was kind of just
poking around, looking

00:32:53.140 --> 00:32:54.200
for a question of the week.

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:56.440
And I've never looked at
Cloud Key Management Service.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:58.481
But basically, it's a
cloud hosted key management

00:32:58.481 --> 00:33:00.670
service that lets you
manage cryptographic keys

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:01.450
for your cloud.

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:03.395
It's actually really cool.

00:33:03.395 --> 00:33:05.020
So there's a couple
of posts that we'll

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:07.810
link to about basically doing
encryption and decryption.

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:09.412
I am not a security
expert, so I'm

00:33:09.412 --> 00:33:11.370
going to try and read
this as much as possible.

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:12.180
But--

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:14.070
MELANIE WARRICK: It's
like, I'm not a doctor.

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:15.110
MARK MANDEL: I'm not
a doctor, exactly.

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:15.605
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:33:15.605 --> 00:33:16.105
[DING]

00:33:16.105 --> 00:33:20.907
- Dr. Mendel, Dr. Mendel, please
report to neurosurgery in OR7.

00:33:20.907 --> 00:33:21.490
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:22.198
I'm not a lawyer.

00:33:22.198 --> 00:33:23.260
I'm none of those things.

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:25.680
But the client APIs as
well as the gcloud tools

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:28.430
support both symmetric
and asymmetric encryption.

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:30.334
So if I get this right,
symmetric algorithms

00:33:30.334 --> 00:33:32.500
use the same key for both
encryption and decryption,

00:33:32.500 --> 00:33:35.080
whereas asymmetric
algorithms use different keys

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:36.700
for encryption and decryption.

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:40.130
And there are different reasons
for using one over the other.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:42.010
It's really quite
straightforward

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:43.990
to do the encryption
and decryption.

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:46.340
Basically, you're
specifying your location,

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:47.890
your key ring, your key.

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:50.642
And then you specify the
text you want to encrypt.

00:33:50.642 --> 00:33:51.850
And it kind of spits it back.

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:54.220
And then to decrypt it, it's
basically doing the same thing

00:33:54.220 --> 00:33:54.845
all over again.

00:33:54.845 --> 00:33:58.449
And all of those keys are
managed by Cloud Key Management

00:33:58.449 --> 00:33:59.740
Service, which is kind of neat.

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:00.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:02.005
MARK MANDEL: And
very similarly, for--

00:34:02.005 --> 00:34:04.440
yeah the RSA, the asynchronous
one-- asymmetric--

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:05.200
words are hard--

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:05.960
MELANIE WARRICK:
Some days they are.

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:06.220
MARK MANDEL: --encryption.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:08.511
And there's also examples
there for different languages

00:34:08.511 --> 00:34:09.139
as well--

00:34:09.139 --> 00:34:13.449
Go, Java, Python for the
asymmetric, and C#, Go Java,

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:15.449
Node.js, Python, Ruby, PHP.

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:17.449
MELANIE WARRICK: Or just
from your command line.

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:18.400
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
you can do it too.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:19.570
It's actually really
straightforward.

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:20.469
I was super-impressed.

